  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 243 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure lingers through Sunday with areas\nof frost expected outside the Champlain Valley tonight with\nclear skies and lighter winds pointing to strong radiational \ncooling. By Sunday, winds shift more southwesterly promoting\nwarming with temperatures back around seasonal averages in the\nlow to mid 60s. \n\nA front approaches Sunday night and will move through early\nMonday bringing some light rainfall mainly towards the Canadian\nborder. Less than 0.25\" is expected with low potential for up to\n0.33\" over upslope areas in the northern Greens. Winds increase\nout of the northwest later Monday with gusts likely in the \n20-30 mph range for many locations especially in the northern \nChamplain Valley and along Lake Champlain. These breezes will \nlinger and slowly decrease through Tuesday. Cold air advection \nsupports temperatures back into the upper 40s to upper 50s \nMonday through Tuesday. Freezing lows are probable Monday and \nTuesday nights outside the Champlain and St Lawrence Valleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Main impactful weather in the days 4 through 7 timeframe \nwill be potential for freezing temperatures daybreak Wednesday where \na strong surface inversion is present. Temperatures remain on track \nbe a garden killer where vegetation remains alive, with exceptions \npotentially in the northern Champlain Valley, particularly in the \nwide valley that is prone to see southerly winds mix to the surface. \nThese winds should develop overnight and moderate temperatures \nsubstantially after initially falling off after sunset, as high \npressure shifts to our southeast overnight. In contrast, \nsouthern/eastern portions of the Champlain and Connecticut River \nValley will be vulnerable to colder conditions and frost; the latest \nprobabilities of freezing temperatures are near 15-30%. Meanwhile, \nthroughout the Adirondack region and the remainder of \ncentral/eastern Vermont, those probabilities are about 40-90%. Where \nwinds remain calm overnight, temperatures could fall up to a few \ndegrees below current forecast values and the lowest of the season \nso far.  \n\nConsensus in the longer range (Wednesday through Friday night) shows \nsomewhat unsettled weather with a closed low near Hudson Bay finally \ndeparting towards the weekend. This scenario holds a longwave trough \nover our region but leaves rain chances higher to our north, and \nlikewise in northern portions of northern New York and Vermont. Low \nlevel flow will initially be southerly leading to a warming trend \nthrough at least Thursday, but a couple of cold fronts will probably \npass by to support a cooling trend towards next weekend. There is \ntypical increasing spread in the temperature range each day, \nassociated with timing and magnitude differences in the air mass \nchange with any front. Overall, temperatures will are expected to be \nfairly typical of this time of year regardless of variability. As we \nget closer to next weekend the details will become sharper with \nregards to these fluctuations.",
